Today, Defense Appropriations Subcommittee Chairman Ken Calvert issued the following statement on America’s national security and the Fiscal Year 2027 Defense Appropriations bill: “The American people sent us here to make the hard choices – both Democrats and Republicans. The recent election was certainly a message on the issues that are impacting Americans the most – inflation, affordability, and the price of gas. Republicans did not deliver on those issues, and I respect the choice of the electorate. However, the election was not a permission slip for the abdication of our duties when it comes to the defense of our country and our national security interests. We need to be clear-eyed about what another Continuing Resolution would mean for our military: Munitions and magazine depth collapse Ongoing operations and prior support to partners have burned through stocks of precision munitions, interceptors, and long-range fires. Multi-year procurement contracts and industrial-base ramp-ups are among the items most dependent on stable or supplemental funding. Prolonged CRs block or delay these, leaving the U.S. and allies short for a high-intensity fight. Readiness and force-generation erosion CRs restrict new starts, limit production-rate increases, and force deferral of maintenance, training, and exercises. Navy ship maintenance availabilities are particularly vulnerable; deferred work compounds into longer future yard periods and lower availability. Flying hours, live-fire training, and large-scale exercises (critical for Indo-Pacific deterrence) suffer. GAO and service leaders have documented schedule delays, higher costs, and reduced readiness from repeated CRs. Extended deployments already strain the fleet; funding uncertainty accelerates the problem. Delayed modernization against the China pacing threat China’s rapid nuclear expansion (projected toward 1,000+ warheads by 2030), conventional buildup, space/counterspace systems, and gray-zone activities in the Indo-Pacific require sustained investment in missile defense, autonomous systems, undersea warfare, long-range fires, and resilient C4ISR/space architectures. CRs prohibit most “new starts” create stop-start uncertainty that raises costs and slows fielding. This directly weakens deterrence and warfighting capacity in the primary theater.⁠ Industrial base and long-term capacity atrophy Unpredictable demand signals deter private investment, slow production lines, raise unit costs, and risk loss of skilled labor and suppliers. Once lost, capacity is expensive and time-consuming to rebuild—exactly when surge production would be needed most. Fragile supply chains are further strained, resulting in a loss of providers further destabilizing the defense industrial base. New companies that are investing in R&D, facilitation, equipment and technology will fail as DOW is prevented from putting new activities on contract. This town is full of excuses for why we cannot pass the funding bills and reconciliation before us. Budget hawks will argue that we cannot add to the debt. I would respond that we must address the true drivers of that debt—and national security spending is not one of them. In fact, defense spending as a share of GDP is near a historic low, exceeded only by the post-Cold War lows of the 1990s. To the Democrats who want more domestic spending to match defense spending, almost 72% of all outlays are on domestic programs, only 13% are for the national defense. The remaining 15% is servicing our debt. We are at an inflection point, do we continue to be the lone world superpower, the reserve currency of the world, or do we choose to accelerate a decline that began in 1990s at the infamous 'Last Supper' event that launched a dire period of drastic industry consolidation? Passing these bills is hard, and it will require negotiation, compromise, and determination. That is what the American people expect of us. It is unconscionable that as we have U.S. servicemembers deployed around the world, Representatives and Senators are all too eager to leave town. Let’s stop making excuses and get to work.” ### Issues : Defense Office Locations Washington DC Office 2205 Rayburn Building Washington, DC 20515 Phone: (202) 225-1986 Fax: (202) 225-2004 Palm Desert Office 73585 Fred Waring Drive Suite 108 Palm Desert, CA 92260 Phone: (760) 620-0041 Corona Office 400 S. Vicentia Avenue Suite 125 Corona, CA 92882 Phone: (951) 277-0042 Fax: (951) 277-0420 const allHomepageWebforms = document.querySelectorAll('#webform-submission-webform-225-node-43-add-form'); if (allHomepageWebforms[0] !== null) { allHomepageWebforms.forEach(form => { form.insertAdjacentHTML('beforebegin', ` `); }) } body > #evo-displayer-background-dim { display: none !important; } Copyright Privacy House.gov Accessibility RSS body[class*="path-"] { background: #fff url(/sites/evo-subsites/calvert.house.gov/files/evo-media-image/homepage-background.jpg) fixed top no-repeat!important; } iframe { border: none; border: 0 } table[aria-label="Recent Votes Table"] thead th { color: #fff; } const evoHomeButton = document.querySelector('#superfish-main'); if (evoHomeButton !== null) { evoHomeButton.insertAdjacentHTML('afterbegin', ` `); } {"path":{"baseUrl":"/","pathPrefix":"","currentPath":"node/4739","currentPathIsAdmin":false,"isFront":false,"currentLanguage":"en"},"pluralDelimiter":"","suppressDeprecationErrors":true,"gtag":{"tagId":"","consentMode":false,"otherIds":[],"events":[],"additionalConfigInfo":[]},"gtm":{"tagId":null,"settings":{"data_layer":"dataLayer","include_environment":false},"tagIds":["GTM-TQX94K2"]},"data":{"extlink":{"extTarget":true,"extTargetAppendNewWindowDisplay":true,"extTargetAppendNewWindowLabel":"(opens in a new window)","extTargetNoOverride":true,"extNofollow":true,"extTitleNoOverride":false,"extNoreferrer":true,"extFollowNoOverride":true,"extClass":"ext","extLabel":"(link is external)","extImgClass":false,"extSubdomains":false,"extExclude":"","extInclude":"",